---
3md: 1.0
axis: layers
---

@plane z=0 label="Context"
The spec stays `specs/merge/merge.spec.md`.

Detects and conservatively auto-resolves git merge conflicts in spec files using context-aware strategies. Only lossless conflict shapes are resolved: known YAML lists are unioned, numeric versions take the maximum value, changelog tables are merged chronologically, and non-conflicting generic table rows are unioned. Ambiguous content leaves the entire file untouched.

This plane is context. Later planes are lessons, in the order they were learned.
`context.md` stays beside this file.

@plane z=1 label="Key Decisions"
- **Section-aware strategies**: `resolve_conflict` dispatches on the enclosing section (last `## ` heading, computed via `detect_section` from text emitted so far). Frontmatter (before any heading) unions known lists, takes the maximum numeric version, and rejects ambiguous scalars; `## Change Log` merges rows chronologically; any section that is *pure table rows* gets a lossless key-based union; everything else (prose) is left for manual resolution.
- **Exact region parsing, not regex precedence**: `parse_conflict_regions` accepts exact `<<<<<<< / ||||||| / ======= / >>>>>>>` forms and separates HEAD, base, and incoming regions. Base content is excluded from auto-resolution inputs; orphan, nested, duplicate, incomplete, and lookalike markers keep the complete file manual.
- **Conservative YAML subset plus checked validation**: `parse_yaml_fields` distinguishes null scalars from empty lists and handles only top-level key/scalar and known-list shapes. Nested mappings remain manual. Every candidate output must contain frontmatter and is checked by the shared YAML parser for duplicate keys and by canonical required-field/status rules.
- **No ambiguous winner**: numeric versions use the maximum value and known frontmatter lists are unioned. Divergent or one-sided non-version scalars, nonnumeric versions, same-key table rows, and table header/separator hunks remain manual; no branch is silently selected.
- **All-or-nothing persistence**: a file is written only after every hunk resolves and frontmatter validates. Auto-resolvable hunks are reported but not persisted when any hunk remains manual.
- **Truthful persistence wording**: dry-run candidates remain `Auto-resolvable`; details become `Auto-resolved` only after the complete file is successfully written.
- **Conservative fallbacks**: when `git diff` fails in git mode, `detect_conflicted_specs` returns `None` and `merge_specs` reports one `Unknown` result (#578), so a scan that never ran is not read as clean; unreadable files become `Manual`; a still-conflicted result keeps its markers.

@plane z=2 label="Files to Read First"
- `src/merge.rs` — entire module: `merge_specs` (driver), `resolve_spec_conflicts` (orchestrator), `parse_conflict_regions`, `resolve_conflict` and the three strategy functions (`resolve_changelog_conflict`, `resolve_table_conflict`, `resolve_frontmatter_conflict`).

@plane z=3 label="Current Status"
CHG-0066 is archived; the implementation it verified is shipped. The public API is `merge_specs`, `has_conflict_markers`, `conflict_hunks`, `conflict_free_side`, `document_conflict_hunks`, `unmerged_paths`, `cached_unmerged_paths`, `print_results`, and `results_to_json`, plus `MergeResult`/`MergeStatus`/`ConflictHunk`/`ConflictSide`.

@plane z=4 label="Notes"
- Depends on `parser::parse_frontmatter` and `parse_checked_issue_references` for post-resolution structural/YAML validation and `validator::find_spec_files` for all-files scan.
- Changelog sorting assumes ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` dates so lexicographic order is chronological.
- `print_results` skips `Clean` entries; `results_to_json` includes all four statuses (`resolved`, `manual`, `clean`, `unknown`).

@plane z=5 label="Lesson (#862)"
Two finalized branches that each bump one module write the same `version: N+1` and append at the same change-log, `files:`, and `requirements.md` lines, so a plain 3-way merge conflicts. The marker resolver cannot count both bumps: a hunk has no ancestor, so it still takes `max(version)` and still refuses prose. `merge_contract` is the git driver (`merge-file %O %A %B %P`). It has the ancestor, so the version is ancestor plus both deltas, lists and change-log rows union, and a new requirement ID that both sides invented is kept twice for `check` to name. Divergent purpose text stays a conflict. `specsync merge --install` records the driver; without that local config, git's ordinary merge is unchanged.

@plane z=6 label="Unknown is not empty (#884)"
#578 made a failed Git discovery an `Unknown` result that exits non-zero, and the spec, invariants and tests said so, but the acceptance list, context, this file and the regression matrix still described the old empty-list no-op and named a test that had been renamed. Two current answers to one question is a contract defect even when the code is right. `merge_contracts_call_a_failed_git_discovery_unknown` now fails if the old wording returns or if testing.md cites a test name that no function declares.

@plane z=7 label="A clean report is a claim (#870 #872 #875 #876)"
The merge driver can be wrong in two ways. A conflict it cannot resolve costs a person a minute. A merge it reports clean but gets wrong costs nothing at the time and surfaces later as a spec that means something else. Four review follow-ups to #865 were all the second kind: a quoted `"123"` rendered plain becomes a number; a blank line skipped inside the change log dropped it from the output and let a later table's rows be sorted and renumbered as change-log rows; a version comment taken from `ours` made the result depend on merge direction; and the `Version` renumbering wrapped past `u64::MAX`. Each fix either keeps both sides exactly or gives up to a conflict. Reject one fix: inventing text neither side wrote, such as joining two comments. When a merge step rewrites a value, ask what a YAML or Markdown reader will make of the rewritten bytes, not only whether they parse. Test both merge orders.
